    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d684">

                <pb facs="0787" n="688" sameAs="frus:frus1936v02/pg688"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">852.00/2921: Circular telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Third Secretary of Embassy in Spain</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Wendelin</hi>)<anchor n="7"
                        xml:id="d684-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-08-31T14:00:00-04:00">August 31, 1936—2
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The unsuccessful attempt of an unidentified plane to bomb the United States
                    destroyer <hi rendition="#italic">Kane</hi> yesterday afternoon approximately 38
                    miles off the extreme southwest coast of Spain suggests that perhaps the armed
                    forces of the two factions in Spain are not familiar with the fact that there
                    are two United States destroyers now operating in waters in the vicinity of
                    Spain for the purpose of evacuating American nationals. These vessels, the U. S.
                    S. <hi rendition="#italic">Hatfield</hi> and the U. S. S. <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Kane</hi>, are relieving the battleship <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Oklahoma</hi> which sailed from Gibraltar this afternoon
                    for the United States. They have four stacks and on the bow of each destroyer is
                    painted in large white numerals the number of the ship. The <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Hatfield</hi> is No. 231 and the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Kane</hi> No. 235.</p>
                <pb facs="0788" n="689" xml:id="pg689"/>
                <p>You are instructed immediately to bring the foregoing information orally and
                    informally to the attention of the military authorities in command in your
                    district, Government or insurgent as the case may be. In so doing you will add
                    that the United States heavy cruiser <hi rendition="#italic">Quincy</hi> and the
                    United States Coast Guard cutter <hi rendition="#italic">Cayuga</hi> have for
                    some weeks been operating in and out of Spanish waters for the purpose of
                    evacuating American nationals. It is assumed that all of the military
                    authorities are familiar with the characteristics of the latter two vessels, but
                    they should be reminded of the continued presence of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Quincy</hi> and the <hi rendition="#italic">Cayuga</hi> in the vicinity of
                    Spain.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d684-f1" n="7" type="source" xml:id="d684-f1-content">
                <p>Sent also to the Consuls at Barcelona, Bilbao, Malaga, Seville, Valencia, and
                    Vigo.</p>
            </note>
